# {{ assembly }}

A C# Revit add-in starter for {{ display_name }}, generated by toolkit_engine {{ foundation_version }}.
These files are managed: regenerate the workspace instead of editing them. Put your own
commands in new files beside them.

It adds one ribbon button, **Hello Add-in**, on the **{{ panel }}** panel of the **{{ tab }}** tab.
The command is read-only (`TransactionMode.ReadOnly`, no `Transaction` anywhere): it checks that a
project document is open, counts the non-template views, and shows a small window styled with the
workspace's generated theme.

## Supported Revit versions

| Revit | Target framework | Status |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2027 | `net10.0-windows` | compiles against the installed API |
| 2026 | `net10.0-windows` for 2026.5 and later, `net8.0-windows` before | compiles against the installed API |
| 2025 | `net10.0-windows` for 2025.5 and later, `net8.0-windows` before | compiles against the installed API |
| 2024 | .NET Framework 4.8 | **not covered**: its reference assemblies are not part of this starter and are not downloaded for you |

Autodesk moved Revit 2025 and 2026 from .NET 8 to .NET 10 in the 2025.5 and 2026.5 updates. An add-in
built for one runtime does not load in a host on the other. For 2025 and 2026 the project reads the
installed product's `AdApplicationFrame.runtimeconfig.json` to pick the framework; pass
`-p:RevitTargetFramework=net8.0-windows` or `net10.0-windows` to override. Any other `RevitVersion`
stops the build with `TKADDIN001`, which names the supported versions.

## Build

Requires a .NET SDK that can target the framework above (the .NET 10 SDK builds every row) and the Revit version installed,
because the add-in compiles against `RevitAPI.dll` and `RevitAPIUI.dll` in the Revit install folder.
The project has no NuGet packages, so it restores offline.

```powershell
dotnet build addins\{{ assembly }}\{{ assembly }}.csproj -c Release -p:RevitVersion=2026
```

- `-p:RevitVersion=2025|2026|2027` selects the Revit API folder and, with the rule above, the target framework.
- `-p:RevitInstallDir="D:\Apps\Revit 2026\"` overrides the default `C:\Program Files\Autodesk\Revit <year>\`.
- Output: `addins\{{ assembly }}\bin\Release\<year>\` (the DLL, plus any packaged fonts under `fonts\`).
  `bin` and `obj` are ignored by the `.gitignore` in this folder.

## Install

1. Close Revit.
2. Copy the contents of `bin\Release\<year>\` to a folder named `{{ assembly }}` inside the Revit add-ins
   folder for that year.
3. Copy `{{ assembly }}.addin` into the add-ins folder itself. Its `<Assembly>` path
   (`{{ assembly }}\{{ assembly }}.dll`) is relative to the folder that holds the `.addin` file.

Add-ins folders:

- Per user (all supported years): `%APPDATA%\Autodesk\Revit\Addins\<year>\`
- All users, Revit 2025 and 2026: `C:\ProgramData\Autodesk\Revit\Addins\<year>\`
- All users, **Revit 2027**: the search location moved to `C:\Program Files\Autodesk\Revit\Addins\2027\`
  (and application plug-ins from `ProgramData` to `C:\Program Files\Autodesk\ApplicationPlugins`).
  Per-user manifests still work.

Revit asks once whether to trust an unsigned add-in on first load.

## Stable identity

- `AddInId` is `{{ addin_id }}`. It is derived from `technical.workspace_id`, so re-rendering or rebranding
  never changes it. Changing `technical.workspace_id` changes it, which Revit sees as a different add-in.
- The assembly is `{{ assembly }}` and the namespace is `{{ root_namespace }}`. Both come from the
  stable technical identity (`technical.pyrevit.extension`, `technical.namespace`), not the display name.
- Display strings (product name, company, window text, `<Name>` in the manifest) follow the firm identity.

## Ribbon and pyRevit

The add-in creates the ribbon tab **{{ tab }}**. A pyRevit extension in this workspace uses a tab with the
same name. Revit tolerates the add-in reusing an existing tab (it catches the "already exists" error and
adds its own panel), but a tab shared by pyRevit and a compiled add-in has not been observed in a live
session. The add-in's panel is named **{{ panel }}** so it never matches a pyRevit panel. If the tab
cannot be shared, the panel falls back to Revit's built-in **Add-Ins** tab.

## Not verified

- A successful `dotnet build` proves the code compiles against the Revit API assemblies installed on
  the build machine. It does **not** prove Revit loads the add-in, that the ribbon appears, or that the
  window renders. Nothing here has been loaded in Revit.
- The theme XAML is parsed at run time (`XamlReader`); it has not been run inside the Revit process.
- Tab sharing with pyRevit, the 2027 all-users location, and the .NET 8 builds (older 2025/2026 hosts) are all untested in a host.
